## sn23.1
sn23.1:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvathī.
sn23.1:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Rādha went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Ven. Rādha went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
sn23.1:2.1 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘Māra’. | T: “‘Māra, Māra,’ it is said, lord.
sn23.1:2.2 | S: How is Māra defined?” | T: To what extent is there said to be Māra?”
sn23.1:2.3 | S: “When there is form, Rādha, there would be Māra, or the murderer, or the murdered. | T: “Rādha, when there is form, there would be Māra (Mortality) or what mortalizes or what is mortalized.
sn23.1:2.4 | S: So you should see form as Māra, the murderer, the murdered, the diseased, the boil, the dart, the gloom, the gloominess. | T: Therefore, Rādha, see form as ‘Māra’ or ‘mortalizing’ or ‘mortalized.’ See it as a disease, a cancer, an arrow, a misery, a great misery.
sn23.1:2.5 | S: Those who see it like this see rightly. | T: Those who see it this way see it rightly.
sn23.1:2.6 | S: When there is feeling … | T: “When there is feeling…
sn23.1:2.7 | S: perception … | T: “When there is perception…
sn23.1:2.8 | S: choices … | T: “When there are fabrications…
sn23.1:2.9 | S: consciousness, there may be Māra, or the murderer, or the murdered. | T: “When there is consciousness, there would be Māra (Mortality) or what mortalizes or what is mortalized.
sn23.1:2.10 | S: So you should see consciousness as Māra, the murderer, the murdered, the diseased, the boil, the dart, the gloom, the gloominess. | T: Therefore, Rādha, see consciousness as ‘Māra’ or ‘mortalizing’ or ‘mortalized.’ See it as a disease, a cancer, an arrow, a misery, a great misery.
sn23.1:2.11 | S: Those who see it like this see rightly.” | T: Those who see it this way see it rightly.
sn23.1:3.1 | S: “But sir, what’s the purpose of seeing rightly?” | T: “But seeing rightly, lord, has what as its purpose?”
sn23.1:3.2 | S: “Disillusionment is the purpose of seeing rightly.” | T: “Seeing rightly, Rādha, has disenchantment as its purpose.”
sn23.1:3.3 | S: “But what’s the purpose of disillusionment?” | T: “And disenchantment, lord, has what as its purpose?”
sn23.1:3.4 | S: “Dispassion is the purpose of disillusionment.” | T: “Disenchantment, Rādha, has dispassion as its purpose.”
sn23.1:3.5 | S: “But what’s the purpose of dispassion?” | T: “And dispassion, lord, has what as its purpose?”
sn23.1:3.6 | S: “Freedom is the purpose of dispassion.” | T: “Dispassion, Rādha, has release as its purpose.”
sn23.1:3.7 | S: “But what’s the purpose of freedom?” | T: “And release, lord, has what as its purpose?”
sn23.1:3.8 | S: “Extinguishment is the purpose of freedom.” | T: “Release, Rādha, has unbinding as its purpose.”
sn23.1:3.9 | S: “But sir, what is the purpose of extinguishment?” | T: “And unbinding, lord, has what as its purpose?”
sn23.1:3.10 | S: “Your question goes too far, Rādha. You weren’t able to grasp the limit of questioning. | T: “The question has gone too far, Rādha. You can’t hold on up to the limit of questions,
sn23.1:3.11 | S: For extinguishment is the objective, destination, and culmination of the spiritual life.” | T: for this holy life is lived with unbinding as its foothold, with unbinding as its culmination, unbinding as its final end.”
sn23.1:3.12 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn23.2
sn23.2:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn23.2:1.2 | S: Seated to one side, Venerable Rādha said to the Buddha: | T: Then Ven. Rādha went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
sn23.2:1.3 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called a ‘sentient being’. | T: “‘A being,’ lord. ‘A being,’ it’s said.
sn23.2:1.4 | S: How is a sentient being defined?” | T: To what extent is one said to be ‘a being’?”
sn23.2:1.5 | S: “Rādha, when you cling, strongly cling, to desire, greed, relishing, and craving for form, then a being is spoken of. | T: “Any desire, passion, delight, or craving for form, Rādha: when one is caught up [satta] there, tied up [visatta] there, one is said to be ‘a being [satta].’
sn23.2:1.6 | S: When you cling, strongly cling, to desire, greed, relishing, and craving for feeling … | T: “Any desire, passion, delight, or craving for feeling…
sn23.2:1.7 | S: perception … | T: perception…
sn23.2:1.8 | S: choices … | T: fabrications…
sn23.2:1.9 | S: consciousness, then a being is spoken of. | T: “Any desire, passion, delight, or craving for consciousness, Rādha: when one is caught up there, tied up there, one is said to be ‘a being.’
sn23.2:2.1 | S: Suppose some boys or girls were playing with sandcastles. | T: “Just as when boys or girls are playing with little sand castles (lit: dirt houses):
sn23.2:2.2 | S: As long as they’re not rid of greed, desire, fondness, thirst, passion, and craving for those sandcastles, they cling to them, dally with them, treasure them, and treat them as their own. | T: as long as they are not free from passion, desire, love, thirst, fever, & craving for those little sand castles, that’s how long they have fun with those sand castles, enjoy them, treasure them, feel possessive of them.
sn23.2:2.3 | S: But when they are rid of greed, desire, fondness, thirst, passion, and craving for those sandcastles, they scatter, smash, and destroy them with their hands and feet, making them unplayable. | T: But when they become free from passion, desire, love, thirst, fever, & craving for those little sand castles, then they smash them, scatter them, demolish them with their hands or feet, and make them unfit for play.
sn23.2:2.4 | S: In the same way, you should scatter, smash, and destroy form, making it unplayable. And you should practice for the ending of craving. | T: “In the same way, Rādha, you too should smash, scatter, & demolish form, and make it unfit for play. Practice for the ending of craving for form.
sn23.2:2.5 | S: You should scatter, smash, and destroy feeling … | T: “You should smash, scatter, & demolish feeling, and make it unfit for play. Practice for the ending of craving for feeling.
sn23.2:2.6 | S: perception … | T: “You should smash, scatter, & demolish perception, and make it unfit for play. Practice for the ending of craving for perception.
sn23.2:2.7 | S: choices … | T: “You should smash, scatter, & demolish fabrications, and make them unfit for play. Practice for the ending of craving for fabrications.
sn23.2:2.8 | S: consciousness, making it unplayable. And you should practice for the ending of craving. | T: “You should smash, scatter, & demolish consciousness, and make it unfit for play. Practice for the ending of craving for consciousness—
sn23.2:2.9 | S: For the ending of craving is extinguishment.” | T: because the ending of craving, Rādha, is unbinding.”
sn23.2:2.10 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn25.1
sn25.1:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn25.1:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, the eye is impermanent, decaying, and perishing. | T: “Monks, the eye is inconstant, changeable, alterable.
sn25.1:1.3 | S: The ear, | T: The ear…
sn25.1:1.4 | S: nose, | T: The nose…
sn25.1:1.5 | S: tongue, | T: The tongue…
sn25.1:1.6 | S: body, | T: The body…
sn25.1:1.7 | S: and mind are impermanent, decaying, and perishing. | T: The mind is inconstant, changeable, alterable.
sn25.1:1.8 | S: Someone who has faith and confidence in these teachings is called a follower by faith. They’ve stepped into the sure path, they’ve stepped into the level of the true person, and they’ve passed beyond the level of the untrue person. | T: “One who has conviction & belief that these phenomena are this way is called a faith-follower: one who has entered the orderliness of rightness, entered the plane of people of integrity, transcended the plane of the run-of-the-mill.
sn25.1:1.9 | S: They can’t do any deed which would make them be reborn in hell, the animal realm, or the ghost realm. | T: He is incapable of doing any deed by which he might be reborn in hell, in the animal womb, or in the realm of hungry ghosts.
sn25.1:1.10 | S: They can’t die without realizing the fruit of stream-entry. | T: He is incapable of passing away until he has realized the fruit of stream-entry.
sn25.1:2.1 | S: Someone who accepts these teachings after deliberating them with a degree of wisdom is called a follower of teachings. They’ve stepped into the sure path, they’ve stepped into the level of the true person, and they’ve passed beyond the level of the untrue person. | T: “One who, after pondering with a modicum of discernment, has accepted that these phenomena are this way is called a Dhamma-follower: one who has entered the orderliness of rightness, entered the plane of people of integrity, transcended the plane of the run-of-the-mill.
sn25.1:2.2 | S: They can’t do any deed which would make them be reborn in hell, the animal realm, or the ghost realm. | T: He is incapable of doing any deed by which he might be reborn in hell, in the animal womb, or in the realm of hungry ghosts.
sn25.1:2.3 | S: They can’t die without realizing the fruit of stream-entry. | T: He is incapable of passing away until he has realized the fruit of stream-entry.
sn25.1:2.4 | S: Someone who understands and sees these principles is called a stream-enterer, not liable to be reborn in the underworld, assured, destined for awakening.” | T: “One who knows and sees that these phenomena are this way is called a stream-enterer, steadfast, never again destined for states of woe, headed for self-awakening.”
sn25.1:2.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

