## sn3.10
sn3.10:1.1 | S: Now at that time a large group of people had been put in shackles by King Pasenadi of Kosala—some in ropes, some in manacles, some in chains. | T: At Sāvatthī. Now on that occasion a great group of people had been put into bondage by King Pasenadi Kosala—some with ropes, some with wooden shackles, some with chains.
sn3.10:2.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then in the early morning, a large number of monks adjusted their under robes and—carrying their bowls & outer robes—went into Sāvatthī for alms.
sn3.10:2.2 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what was happening. | T: Having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to the Blessed One,
sn3.10:2.3 | P: “idha, bhante, raññā pasenadinā kosalena mahājanakāyo bandhāpito, appekacce rajjūhi appekacce andūhi appekacce saṅkhalikāhī”ti. | T: “Lord, a great group of people has now been put into bondage by King Pasenadi Kosala—some with ropes, some with wooden shackles, some with chains.”
sn3.10:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha recited these verses: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion spoke these verses:
sn3.10:4.1 | S: “The attentive say that shackle is not strong | T: That’s not a strong bond —so say the enlightened—
sn3.10:4.2 | S: that’s made of iron, wood, or knots. | T: the one made of iron, of wood, or of grass.
sn3.10:4.3 | S: But obsession with jeweled earrings, | T: To be smitten, enthralled, with jewels & ornaments,
sn3.10:4.4 | S: concern for your partners and children: | T: longing for children & wives:
sn3.10:5.1 | S: this, say the attentive, is a strong shackle | T: That’s the strong bond, —so say the enlightened—
sn3.10:5.2 | S: dragging the indulgent down, hard to escape. | T: one that’s constraining, elastic, hard to untie.
sn3.10:5.3 | S: Having cut this one too they go forth, | T: But having cut it, they —the enlightened—go forth,
sn3.10:5.4 | S: unconcerned, having given up sensual pleasures.” | T: free of longing, abandoning sensual ease.
sn3.10:5.5 | P: Paṭhamo vaggo. | T: (none)
sn3.10:6.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn3.10:6.1 | P: Daharo puriso jarā, | T: (none)
sn3.10:6.2 | P: piyaṁ attānarakkhito; | T: (none)
sn3.10:6.3 | P: Appakā aḍḍakaraṇaṁ, | T: (none)
sn3.10:6.4 | P: mallikā yaññabandhananti. | T: (none)

## sn3.13
sn3.13:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Dwelling at Sāvatthī…
sn3.13:1.2 | S: Now at that time King Pasenadi of Kosala used to eat boiled rice by the bucketful. | T: Now, on that occasion King Pasenadi of Kosala ate a gallon-measure of rice.
sn3.13:1.3 | S: Then after eating King Pasenadi of Kosala went up to the Buddha, huffing and puffing. He bowed and sat down to one side. | T: Then, having just finished his meal and breathing heavily, he went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
sn3.13:2.1 | S: Then, knowing that King Pasenadi was huffing and puffing after eating, on that occasion the Buddha spoke this verse: | T: Then the Blessed One, sensing that King Pasenadi of Kosala had just finished his meal and was breathing heavily, recited this verse:
sn3.13:3.1 | S: “When a man is always mindful, | T: For a person always mindful,
sn3.13:3.2 | S: knowing moderation in eating, | T: knowing moderation in the food he’s obtained,
sn3.13:3.3 | S: his discomfort fades, | T: his pains grow slender;
sn3.13:3.4 | S: and he ages slowly, taking care of his life.” | T: gradually he ages, guarding his life.
sn3.13:4.1 | S: Now at that time the student Sudassana was standing behind the king. | T: Now, on that occasion the young brahman Sudassana was standing behind King Pasenadi of Kosala.
sn3.13:4.2 | S: Then King Pasenadi addressed him, | T: So the king addressed him,
sn3.13:4.3 | S: “Please, dear Sudassana, memorize this verse in the Buddha’s presence and recite it to me whenever I am served a meal. | T: “Come now, my dear Sudassana. Having learned this verse in the Blessed One’s presence, recite it during the time I am taking my meal,
sn3.13:4.4 | S: I’ll set up a regular daily allowance of a hundred dollars for you.” | T: and I will bestow on you a daily stipend of one hundred kahapaṇa.”
sn3.13:4.5 | S: “Yes, Your Majesty,” replied Sudassana. He memorized that verse in the Buddha’s presence, and then whenever the king was served a meal he would repeat it: | T: Saying, “As you say, your majesty,” the young brahman Sudassana and learning the verse in the Blessed One’s presence, he recited it during the time when King Pasenadi was taking his meal:
sn3.13:5.1 | S: “When a man is always mindful, | T: For a person always mindful,
sn3.13:5.2 | S: knowing moderation in eating, | T: knowing moderation in the food he’s obtained,
sn3.13:5.3 | S: his discomfort fades, | T: his pains grow slender;
sn3.13:5.4 | S: and he ages slowly, taking care of his life.” | T: gradually he ages, guarding his life.
sn3.13:6.1 | S: Then the king gradually got used to having at most a cup of rice. | T: Then King Pasenadi of Kosala step-by-step restrained himself to at most a quart-measure of rice.
sn3.13:6.2 | S: After some time King Pasenadi’s body slimmed right down. Stroking his limbs with his hands, at that time he expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: At a later time, when he had become quite slim, he stroked his limbs with his hand and exclaimed,
sn3.13:6.3 | S: “In both ways the Buddha has sympathy for me: | T: “O, how the Blessed One has shown sympathy for my benefit of both sorts:
sn3.13:6.4 | S: in the good of this life and the good of lives to come.” | T: for that in the present life, and that in the life to come!”

## sn3.14
sn3.14:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī.
sn3.14:1.2 | S: Then King Ajātasattu of Magadha, son of the princess of Videha, mobilized an army of four divisions and marched to Kāsi to attack King Pasenadi of Kosala. | T: Then King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the son of Queen Videha, raising a fourfold army, marched toward Kāsi against King Pasenadi Kosala.
sn3.14:1.3 | S: When King Pasenadi heard of this, | T: King Pasenadi heard,
sn3.14:1.4 | P: “rājā kira māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto caturaṅginiṁ senaṁ sannayhitvā mamaṁ abbhuyyāto yena kāsī”ti. | T: “King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the son of Queen Videha, they say, has raised a fourfold army and is marching toward Kāsi against me.”
sn3.14:1.5 | S: he mobilized an army of four divisions and marched to Kāsi to defend it against Ajātasattu. | T: So King Pasenadi, raising a fourfold army, launched a counter-attack toward Kāsi against King Ajātasattu.
sn3.14:1.6 | S: Then the two kings met in battle. | T: Then King Ajātasattu & King Pasenadi fought a battle,
sn3.14:1.7 | S: And in that battle Ajātasattu defeated Pasenadi, | T: and in that battle King Ajātasattu defeated King Pasenadi.
sn3.14:1.8 | S: who withdrew to his own capital at Sāvatthī. | T: King Pasenadi, defeated, marched back to his capital at Sāvatthī.
sn3.14:2.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then in the early morning, a large number of monks, having adjusted their lower robes, and taking their bowls & outer robes, went into Sāvatthī for alms.
sn3.14:2.2 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. Then the Buddha said: | T: Having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to the Blessed One:
sn3.14:3.1 | P: “Idha, bhante, rājā māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto caturaṅginiṁ senaṁ sannayhitvā rājānaṁ pasenadiṁ kosalaṁ abbhuyyāsi yena kāsi. | T: “Just now, lord, King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the son of Queen Videha, raising a fourfold army, marched toward Kāsi against King Pasenadi Kosala.
sn3.14:3.2 | P: Assosi kho, bhante, rājā pasenadi kosalo: | T: King Pasenadi heard,
sn3.14:3.3 | P: ‘rājā kira māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto caturaṅginiṁ senaṁ sannayhitvā mamaṁ abbhuyyāto yena kāsī’ti. | T: ‘King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the son of Queen Videha, they say, has raised a fourfold army and is marching toward Kāsi against me.’
sn3.14:3.4 | P: Atha kho, bhante, rājā pasenadi kosalo caturaṅginiṁ senaṁ sannayhitvā rājānaṁ māgadhaṁ ajātasattuṁ vedehiputtaṁ paccuyyāsi yena kāsi. | T: So King Pasenadi, raising a fourfold army, launched a counter-attack toward Kāsi against King Ajātasattu.
sn3.14:3.5 | P: Atha kho, bhante, rājā ca māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto rājā ca pasenadi kosalo saṅgāmesuṁ. | T: Then King Ajātasattu & King Pasenadi fought a battle,
sn3.14:3.6 | P: Tasmiṁ kho pana, bhante, saṅgāme rājā māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto rājānaṁ pasenadiṁ kosalaṁ parājesi. | T: and in that battle King Ajātasattu defeated King Pasenadi.
sn3.14:3.7 | P: Parājito ca, bhante, rājā pasenadi kosalo sakameva rājadhāniṁ sāvatthiṁ paccuyyāsī”ti. | T: King Pasenadi, defeated, marched back to his capital at Sāvatthī.”
sn3.14:4.1 | S: “Mendicants, King Ajātasattu has bad friends, companions, and associates. | T: “Monks, King Ajātasattu has evil friends, evil comrades, evil companions,
sn3.14:4.2 | S: But King Pasenadi has good friends, companions, and associates. | T: whereas King Pasenadi has fine friends, fine comrades, fine companions.
sn3.14:4.3 | S: Yet on this day King Pasenadi will have a bad night’s sleep as one defeated.” | T: Yet for now, King Pasenadi will lie down tonight in pain, defeated.”
sn3.14:4.4 | S: That is what the Buddha said. … | T: That is what the Blessed One said. Having said that, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, said further:
sn3.14:5.1 | S: “Victory breeds enmity; | T: “Winning gives birth to hostility.
sn3.14:5.2 | S: the defeated sleep badly. | T: Losing, one lies down in pain.
sn3.14:5.3 | S: The peaceful sleep at ease, | T: The calmed lie down with ease,
sn3.14:5.4 | S: having left victory and defeat behind.” | T: having set winning & losing aside.”

## sn3.15
sn3.15:1.1 | S: Then King Ajātasattu of Magadha, son of the princess of Videha, mobilized an army of four divisions and marched to Kāsi to attack King Pasenadi of Kosala. | T: Staying near Sāvatthī. Then King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the son of Queen Videha, raising a fourfold army, marched toward Kāsi against King Pasenadi Kosala.
sn3.15:1.2 | S: When King Pasenadi heard of this, | T: King Pasenadi heard,
sn3.15:1.3 | P: “rājā kira māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto caturaṅginiṁ senaṁ sannayhitvā mamaṁ abbhuyyāto yena kāsī”ti. | T: “King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the son of Queen Videha, they say, has raised a fourfold army and is marching toward Kāsi against me.”
sn3.15:1.4 | S: he mobilized an army of four divisions and marched to Kāsi to defend it against Ajātasattu. | T: So King Pasenadi, raising a fourfold army, launched a counter-attack toward Kāsi against King Ajātasattu.
sn3.15:1.5 | S: Then the two kings met in battle. | T: Then King Ajātasattu & King Pasenadi fought a battle,
sn3.15:1.6 | S: And in that battle Pasenadi defeated Ajātasattu and captured him alive. | T: and in that battle King Pasenadi defeated King Ajātasattu and captured him alive.
sn3.15:1.7 | S: Then King Pasenadi thought, | T: The thought then occurred to King Pasenadi:
sn3.15:1.8 | S: “Even though I’ve never betrayed this King Ajātasattu, he betrayed me. Still, he is my nephew. | T: “Even though King Ajātasattu has wronged me when I have done him no wrong, still he is my nephew.
sn3.15:1.9 | S: Now that I’ve vanquished all of Ajātasattu’s elephant troops, cavalry, chariots, and infantry, why don’t I let him loose with just his life?” | T: What if I, having confiscated all his elephant troops, all his cavalry, all his chariots, & all his infantry, were to let him go with just his life?”
sn3.15:2.1 | S: And that’s what he did. | T: So King Pasenadi—having confiscated all his elephant troops, cavalry, chariots, & infantry—let King Ajātasattu go with just his life.
sn3.15:3.1 | S: Then several mendicants … | T: Then in the early morning, a large number of monks, having adjusted their lower robes and taking their bowls & outer robes, went into Sāvatthī for alms. Having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
sn3.15:3.2 | S: told the Buddha what had happened. | T: As they were sitting there, they [reported these events to the Blessed One].
sn3.15:4.1 | P: “Idha, bhante, rājā māgadho ajātasattu vedehiputto caturaṅginiṁ senaṁ sannayhitvā rājānaṁ pasenadiṁ kosalaṁ abbhuyyāsi yena kāsi. | T: (none)

